WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.076
Returning a reference is safe only when
the referenced data will remain valid for

2
00:00:04.156 --> 00:00:04.681
the caller.

3
00:00:04.761 --> 00:00:08.127
That means the caller must have access to
an owner that outlives the returned

4
00:00:08.207 --> 00:00:08.732
reference.

5
00:00:08.812 --> 00:00:10.916
If the owner disappears when the function
ends,

6
00:00:10.996 --> 00:00:13.309
the reference cannot be valid outside the
function.

7
00:00:13.389 --> 00:00:16.807
A function cannot safely return a
reference to a local temporary value that is

8
00:00:16.887 --> 00:00:18.570
cleaned up when the function returns.

9
00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:22.278
Rust rejects this pattern in safe code
because it would give the caller a reference

10
00:00:22.358 --> 00:00:23.673
to data that no longer exists.

11
00:00:23.753 --> 00:00:25.803
That rejection is a feature, not a
limitation.

12
00:00:25.883 --> 00:00:28.144
Returned references often borrow from
input data.

13
00:00:28.224 --> 00:00:31.222
In that design, the function is not
creating a new long-lived value.

14
00:00:31.302 --> 00:00:34.825
It is selecting, slicing, or exposing part
of data that the caller already owns.

15
00:00:34.905 --> 00:00:38.638
The signature should make that
relationship clear so the caller knows the reference

16
00:00:38.718 --> 00:00:39.612
is tied to the input.

17
00:00:39.692 --> 00:00:42.216
This is common in defensive parsing and
validation code.

18
00:00:42.296 --> 00:00:45.188
A helper might return a borrowed view into
a trusted input buffer,

19
00:00:45.268 --> 00:00:47.529
but only if the caller continues to own
that buffer.

20
00:00:47.609 --> 00:00:50.290
If the helper builds a normalized value,
stores it for later,

21
00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:51.895
or sends it across a task boundary,

22
00:00:51.975 --> 00:00:54.184
an owned return is usually easier to
reason about.

23
00:00:54.264 --> 00:00:56.788
If no valid owner will remain after the
function returns,

24
00:00:56.868 --> 00:00:58.971
the function should return owned data
instead.

25
00:00:59.051 --> 00:01:01.943
That may involve constructing a new
string, object, collection,

26
00:01:02.023 --> 00:01:03.758
or result value that the caller can own.

27
00:01:03.838 --> 00:01:07.624
Returning owned data is often simpler and
safer than forcing a borrowed relationship

28
00:01:07.704 --> 00:01:09.282
that does not match the real design.

29
00:01:09.362 --> 00:01:11.938
This is a major security benefit of Rust's
lifetime model.

30
00:01:12.018 --> 00:01:14.384
Safe Rust prevents stale references before
runtime.

31
00:01:14.464 --> 00:01:16.462
In review, the question is
straightforward:

32
00:01:16.542 --> 00:01:18.751
what owns the data behind the returned
reference,

33
00:01:18.831 --> 00:01:21.460
and can that owner truly outlive every use
of the reference?
